6. Bean Scopes



65. What is bean scope?
-->Bean scope defines how long a Spring Bean's object should live and how many objects Spring should create for that bean.

In simple words:

Bean Scope tells Spring: "How many objects of this bean should I create, and how long should I keep them?"

For example:
    
    @Component
    public class Student {
    }
    
    By default, Spring creates one object of Student.

But we can change the scope:

    @Scope("prototype")
    @Component
    public class Student {
    }

Now Spring can create a new object whenever the bean is requested.

Interview answer :
      Bean scope determines the lifecycle and number of instances created for a Spring bean.  









66. What are bean scopes in Spring?
-->Spring provides these commonly discussed scopes:
                                        Core scopes
                                        Singleton
                                        Prototype
                                        Web-aware scopes
                                        Request
                                        Session
                                        Application
                                        WebSocket

You may also see:
      Global Session — this was associated with older Portlet-based web applications and is generally not relevant to modern Spring Boot
      applications.

        For your interview list, focus especially on:
                                            Singleton
                                            Prototype
                                            Request
                                            Session
                                            Global Session







Core Scopes

67. What is Singleton scope (default)?
-->Singleton is the default scope in Spring.

It means:
      Spring creates only one bean instance per Spring IoC container for that bean definition.

Example:
      @Component
      @Scope("singleton")
      public class StudentService {
      }

Suppose you inject it into two classes:
      @Autowired
      StudentService service1;

and:

      @Autowired
      StudentService service2;

Both references point to the same Spring-managed object.



Conceptually:

        Spring Container
               |
               v
         StudentService Object
               ^
               |
          +----+----+
          |         |
        service1  service2



Important:
----------
Singleton does not mean:
      "Only one object can ever exist in the entire Java application."

It means one instance per Spring IoC container for that bean definition.

Interview answer
----------------
        Singleton is the default Spring bean scope. Spring creates one instance of the bean per IoC container and reuses that instance
        wherever the bean is injected.














68. What is Prototype scope?
-->Prototype means:
            Spring creates a new bean instance each time the bean is requested from the Spring container.

Example:

      @Component
      @Scope("prototype")
      public class Student {
      }

If you request it multiple times:

          Student s1 = context.getBean(Student.class);
          Student s2 = context.getBean(Student.class);

Then:
      s1 → Student Object 1
      
      s2 → Student Object 2

So:

      s1 != s2
      Interview answer
      
      Prototype scope tells Spring to create a new bean instance every time the bean is requested from the container.








Web Scopes

69. What is Request scope?
-->Request scope is used in web applications.

It means:

One bean instance is created for each HTTP request.

Suppose:
--------
@Component
@Scope("request")
public class RequestData {
}

Imagine users send:
--------------------
    Request 1
    Request 2
    Request 3

Spring creates:
---------------
      Request 1 → RequestData Object 1
      
      Request 2 → RequestData Object 2
      
      Request 3 → RequestData Object 3

So each HTTP request gets its own bean.

Example use:
------------
Suppose you want to store information specific to the current request:
            Request ID
            User information
            Request-specific data
            
            Request scope can be useful.
            
            Interview answer
            ----------------
            Request scope creates one bean instance for each HTTP request. The instance exists only during that request.









70. What is Session scope?
-->Session scope is also used in web applications.

It means:
----------
      One bean instance is created for each HTTP session.
      
      Imagine a user logs into your website.
      
      Their session might last for several requests:

User
 ↓
Request 1
Request 2
Request 3
Request 4

All those requests from that session can use the same session-scoped bean:

Session
   |
   v
Cart Object
   |
   +-- Request 1
   +-- Request 2
   +-- Request 3
   +-- Request 4

Example
-------
        A shopping cart is a classic example.
        
        @Component
        @Scope("session")
        public class ShoppingCart {
        }
        
        The user's cart can remain associated with their session.

Interview answer
----------------
    Session scope creates one bean instance per HTTP session. The same instance is used throughout that user's session.









71. What is Global Session scope?
-->This is an older web scope associated with Portlet applications.

It means:

A bean instance is created for the lifecycle of a global HTTP session in a Portlet environment.

You generally won't use this in a normal modern Spring Boot REST application.

Interview answer

Global session scope creates one bean instance for the lifecycle of a global session, mainly in Portlet-based web applications. 
It is not commonly used in modern Spring Boot applications.











Advanced
----------
72. Why singleton is default scope?
-->This is an important interview question.

Spring uses Singleton by default because many application components are stateless services.

For example:
------------
        @Service
        public class UserService {
        
            public User getUser() {
                // logic
            }
        }

The service usually doesn't need its own separate object for every request.

Instead:
--------
                 Spring Container
                       |
                       v
                  UserService
                       |
          +------------+------------+
          |            |            |
       Request 1    Request 2    Request 3

One reusable object is generally enough.

This can reduce unnecessary object creation and allows Spring to manage shared dependencies efficiently.

Interview answer
-----------------
Singleton is the default because most Spring beans, such as services and repositories, are stateless and can safely be shared.
It avoids unnecessary object creation and allows efficient dependency management.











73. Difference between singleton and prototype?
-->
| Singleton                         | Prototype                                                                              |
| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Default scope                     | Not default                                                                            |
| One instance per Spring container | New instance each time the bean is requested                                           |
| Same object reused                | Different objects                                                                      |
| Suitable for stateless services   | Suitable when separate object state is needed                                          |
| Spring manages the bean lifecycle | Spring creates the object, but after creation its lifecycle management is more limited |

Example:

Singleton
----------
    @Component
    @Scope("singleton")
    class A {
    }
    getBean() → Object A
    getBean() → Object A
    getBean() → Object A
    
    Same object.

Prototype
----------
      @Component
      @Scope("prototype")
      class B {
      }
      getBean() → Object B1
      getBean() → Object B2
      getBean() → Object B3
      
      Different objects.

Easy memory trick
-----------------
      Singleton  → SAME
      Prototype  → NEW











74. Which scope is used for shopping cart?
-->For a typical web application, a shopping cart is commonly associated with Session scope, because the cart belongs to a particular
    user's session.

@Component
@Scope("session")
public class ShoppingCart {
}  

Conceptually:

User A
   ↓
Session A
   ↓
Cart A

User B
   ↓
Session B
   ↓
Cart B

Each user gets their own cart.

Interview answer
-----------------
Session scope is commonly used for a shopping cart because the cart needs to persist across multiple requests belonging to the same
user's session.

Note: Modern applications may also store carts in a database, Redis, or another external store, especially when persistence across
devices/sessions is required.











75. Can singleton use prototype bean?
-->Yes.

A Singleton bean can depend on a Prototype bean.

But there is an important problem.

Consider:

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class PrototypeBean {
}

And:

@Component
public class SingletonBean {

    @Autowired
    private PrototypeBean prototypeBean;
}

SingletonBean is created once.

When Spring creates the Singleton:

SingletonBean
      |
      v
PrototypeBean Object 1

That prototype object is injected into the singleton.

Now suppose you use the singleton 100 times:

Request 1 → Singleton → Prototype Object 1
Request 2 → Singleton → Prototype Object 1
Request 3 → Singleton → Prototype Object 1

You might expect:

Request 1 → Prototype Object 1
Request 2 → Prototype Object 2
Request 3 → Prototype Object 3

But simple field injection does not do that.

The prototype bean is resolved when the singleton is created, so the singleton keeps that injected instance.

How can we get a new Prototype object each time?

There are several solutions.

One common solution is ObjectProvider:

@Component
public class SingletonBean {

    @Autowired
    private ObjectProvider<PrototypeBean> provider;

    public void test() {

        PrototypeBean p1 = provider.getObject();
        PrototypeBean p2 = provider.getObject();
    }
}

Now:

provider.getObject()
       ↓
Prototype Object 1

provider.getObject()
       ↓
Prototype Object 2
Interview answer

Yes, a Singleton bean can depend on a Prototype bean. However, if the prototype is directly injected into the singleton, the same prototype instance will remain in that singleton. To obtain a new prototype instance whenever needed, mechanisms such as ObjectProvider, Provider, or method injection can be used.

