Keep this diagram in your mind:

                 SPRING BEAN SCOPES
                         |
          +--------------+--------------+
          |                             |
       Core scopes                  Web scopes
          |                             |
    +-----+-----+             +---------+---------+
    |           |             |         |         |
Singleton   Prototype      Request   Session   Application
    |           |             |
    |           |             |
 SAME object   NEW object   One object
 per container every time  per request

The most important four for your interviews:
        Singleton
           ↓
        One object per Spring container
        
        Prototype
           ↓
        New object whenever requested
        
        Request
           ↓
        One object per HTTP request
        
        Session
           ↓
        One object per HTTP session

And remember the relationship with what you asked previously:
          @Component
               ↓
          Spring creates bean
               ↓
          Default scope = Singleton
               ↓
          One object reused



You can also combine them:
        @Component
        @Scope("prototype")
        public class MyService {
        }

So @Component tells Spring WHAT should become a bean, while @Scope tells Spring HOW MANY instances/lifecycle behavior that bean should have.
